  "privacyHeading": "What this app does with your data",
  "privacyOnDeviceTitle": "Everything stays on this phone",
  "privacyOnDeviceBody": "This app has no internet permission, and a check on every release refuses to build one that asks for it. There is no account to sign into, no sync, and it collects no analytics or crash reports. What you record stays here until you export it.",
  "privacyNotEncryptedTitle": "The database is not encrypted",
  "privacyNotEncryptedBody": "Your data sits in this app's own storage. Other apps cannot read it on a phone that has not been tampered with, and Android's full-device encryption protects it while the phone is switched off. But it is stored as plain text, so anyone who picks up your unlocked phone, or takes a forensic copy of it, can read it. The app lock hides the screen. It does not encrypt the file underneath.",
  "privacyBackupTitle": "Nothing is backed up to the cloud",
  "privacyBackupBody": "Android's automatic backup is switched off for this app, so your cycle history is never copied to a Google account. If you lose the phone, the data is gone unless you exported it.",
  "privacyDeletionTitle": "Deleting really deletes",
  "privacyDeletionBody": "Deleting a day removes the row. Deleting everything removes every row and then reclaims the space, so the old entries are not left readable in the file. Nothing is kept behind a hidden flag.",
  "privacyLogsTitle": "The activity log holds no cycle content",
  "privacyLogsBody": "This app keeps a short log of what it did, such as \"a prediction was computed\" with a count of cycles. You can read it and clear it in Settings.",
  "privacyExportTitle": "Exports are yours to look after",
  "privacyExportBody": "An exported backup is a plain readable file wherever you chose to put it, with no protection of its own. Anywhere you save it or send it is outside this app's control.",

  "bootstrapRecoveredTitle": "Your data could not be read",
  "bootstrapRecoveredBody": "The stored file was damaged, so this app started with an empty one. The damaged file is still on the device. If you have a backup, restoring it now is the best way forward.",
